  "final": "The CSV and JSON exporters now share one pipeline in the new `src/exporter.py`, and `python3 checks/public.py` passes. That script holds the original asserts plus my new ones.\n\n- **Shared entry point:** `export_rows(rows, format)` takes `'csv'` or `'json'` and raises `ValueError(\"unsupported format\")` for anything else, including unhashable values like a list.\n- **Normalization:** it keeps only `id` and `name`, in that order. Missing or `None` values become `''` and everything else goes through `str()`. It builds new dicts, so input rows aren't modified, and it works on one-shot iterables.\n- **Serialization:** CSV uses LF line endings, standard quoting, and an `id,name` header even with no rows. JSON uses `ensure_ascii=False` and `separators=(',', ':')`.\n- **Wrappers:** `export_csv` and `export_json` are now one-line calls to `export_rows`, with their own loops removed.\n- **Dispatcher:** `src/dispatcher.py` still calls the two wrappers, now through a lookup table. It raises the same `ValueError(\"unsupported format\")`, and it also does so for unhashable format values, which would have raised a different error before.\n- **New checks:** in `checks/public.py` I added asserts for the following:\n  - wrapper output matching `export_rows`\n  - quoting, newlines and non-ASCII characters\n  - missing and `None` fields\n  - extra keys being dropped\n  - inputs staying unmodified\n  - generator and iterator inputs\n  - empty input\n  - invalid formats through both entry points",
